{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/adr/2026-11-12-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-and-benefit.md'","aggregated_output":"# ADR: Closeout review policy by cost and benefit\n\n## Status\nAccepted — the maintainer approved this base explicitly on 2026-11-12, including\nlegacy compatibility and `always` requiring a one-member milestone's own review.\nThis records the architectural decision, not policy adoption or a delivery attestation.\n\n## Context\n\nSpec [pose-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-benefit](../specs/2026-11-11-pose-closeout-review-policy-by-cost-benefit.md)\nseparates the mandatory close of an aggregate scope from its independent review.\nThe current [one-member milestone rule](2026-11-02-a-milestone-of-one-member-is-that-member.md)\ninherits the member's approval, while multi-member milestones and roadmaps normally\nrequire their own reviews. Review value depends on composition; the maintainer\nchose explicit deterministic thresholds instead of a judgment made by an agent.\n\nSealed bundles include policy and review plans. A new setting must not reinterpret\nthe historical contract, erase a rejection, skip member reviews, or infer adoption\nfrom an unrecognized optional field.\n\n## Decision\n\nStore `milestone_review_required` and `roadmap_review_required` in the versioned\ninstance review policy `.pose/policy/review.json`. Accept exactly `never`,\n`when-justified`, and `always`; reject unknown values before preparing a current\nplan or writing a closeout. Use one domain evaluator across CLI, MCP, planning,\nverification, state, closeout and federation; expose its reason in their projections.\n\n- `never`: require no independent review of that aggregate scope.\n- `always`: require the scope's own independent review, including a one-member\n  milestone. An inherited member approval does not satisfy this explicit choice.\n- `when-justified`, milestone: require review exactly when it has more than two\n  distinct canonical spec members. Duplicate aliases do not increase the count.\n- `when-justified`, roadmap: require review exactly when its union of milestones\n  contains more than one distinct canonical spec and at least one resolved ADR\n  association. Count the spec once even if it appears in multiple milestones.\n\nRecognize an ADR association declared by the roadmap or a canonical association\nback from an ADR. Resolve explicit references, not incidental words in prose.\nUnknown, unreadable or ambiguous member/association inputs cannot yield an\nexemption: report the missing knowledge and refuse the close until resolved.\nAcceptance tests must fix the reference syntax in the implementation spec before\nchanging its parser.\n\nThe defaults for newly installed/adopting instances are `never` for both keys.\nIntroduce a review-policy schema version that older engines reject. A legacy\npolicy with neither key retains its legacy semantics until explicit adoption;\nsetup/settings show the effective legacy rule and the proposed new values before\nwriting both keys. Do not silently inject `always` (which differs for one-member\nmilestones), and do not silently disable review on an existing instance by parsing\nan absent field as `never`. A partially configured adopting policy is invalid.\n\nAfter adoption, `pose close` remains an explicit operation for every milestone\nand roadmap in all three modes, including scopes whose review is inherited or\ndispensed with. Persist a closeout receipt containing `review: not-required`,\nthe exact policy key/value, evaluator version and reason/counts when exempt.\nSatisfying member reviews or reaching the last member does not itself perform\nthis close. Member reviews and edge/exit-gate validation remain required.\n\nOptional reviews use the same verification as required reviews. An authentic\nnegative review retains its veto until remediated under the existing rules;\nchanging a setting cannot erase it. No exemption may weaken a stronger protected\nbaseline or mandatory-human requirement. `doctor` reports open scopes newly owing\nreview after policy or composition changes; the close gate enforces the result.\n\nVerify scopes already sealed as done against their sealed policy and plan,\nincluding legacy inherited milestones. Never backfill a fictitious exemption,\nreinterpret an existing receipt, or invalidate historical approval merely because\nthe current setting differs. Apply the new close and exemption rules prospectively.\n\n## Consequences\n\nNew instances skip aggregate reviews by default while still closing each scope\nand recording why review was not required. `when-justified` preserves review for\nthe compositions the maintainer selected. Explicit `always` adds cost even for\na one-member milestone; that is deliberate and visible.\n\nImplementation touches review-policy loading, common scope evaluation, persisted\ncloseout evidence, setup/settings, doctor, CLI/MCP projections and their tests.\nThe existing one-member ADR is superseded only for newly adopting open scopes;\nits sealed historical outcomes remain valid. No automatic instance adoption,\nhuman approval or delivery attestation is implied by this proposed ADR.\n\nRejected alternatives:\n\n- Boolean switches: cannot express the requested middle mode.\n- An agent estimates the benefit: yields irreproducible gates.\n- Require review with two milestone members: contradicts the chosen threshold.\n- Default existing missing fields directly to `never`: silently changes authority.\n- Treat `always` as legacy behavior: hides the one-member exception.\n- Ignore a recorded rejection after switching to `never`: launders negative evidence.\n- Recompute done scopes using current policy: breaks historical sealed approvals.\n- Remove close when review is exempt: conflates lifecycle and review requirements.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/rules/backend-go.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/rules/backend-go.md'","aggregated_output":"# Rule: Backend Go\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for HTTP handlers, domain services, persistence, concurrency, and integrations between Go services.\n\n## Required patterns\n\n- Handle and propagate errors with enough context for diagnosis.\n- Validate handler input and return HTTP status codes consistent with the contract.\n- Keep business rules in service or domain layers, not in transport code.\n- Honor `context.Context` for timeouts and cancellation.\n- Put data access behind clear interfaces that support testing.\n- Use structured logs without exposing sensitive data.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Ignoring error returns, including `_` for critical errors.\n- Using `panic` in ordinary request flows instead of controlled error handling.\n- Coupling handlers directly to concrete repository implementations without an abstraction.\n- Running unbounded or unpaginated queries in potentially large endpoints.\n- Introducing concurrency without adequate synchronization or race protection.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run `go test ./...` in the affected scope.\n- Run `go test -race ./...` when concurrency changes.\n- Run `go vet ./...` without blocking findings.\n- Run the applicable Go lint check, such as `golangci-lint`, without critical errors.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/rules/security.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/rules/security.md'","aggregated_output":"# Rule: Security\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for authentication, authorization, sensitive data, external integrations, dependencies, and attack surfaces.\n\n## Required patterns\n\n- Apply least privilege to resource and credential access.\n- Store secrets only in appropriate secret-management mechanisms, never in code.\n- Validate and sanitize external input for its execution context.\n- Keep personal and confidential data out of plaintext logs and metrics.\n- Evaluate new dependencies for maintenance, licensing, and known vulnerabilities.\n- Test both positive and negative authentication and authorization cases.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Committing credentials, tokens, keys, or secrets in any versioned artifact.\n- Disabling TLS or certificate verification without a formally documented mitigation.\n- Trusting client input for authorization decisions.\n- Executing dynamic commands without adequate validation and escaping.\n- Ignoring critical security alerts without an approved exception record.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run secret scanning on the changed diff or scope.\n- Run the module's applicable dependency vulnerability scanner.\n- Pass the relevant authentication and authorization tests.\n- Complete a review for sensitive-data exposure in logs and configuration.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/rules/documentation-style.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/rules/documentation-style.md'","aggregated_output":"# Rule: Documentation Style\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for process documentation, rules, specs, workflows, and operational instructions.\n\n## Required conventions\n\n- Write instructions in the imperative mood and begin them with action verbs.\n- Keep bullets short and limited to one idea.\n- Avoid duplicating sections with the same purpose across files.\n- Link to the single source of truth instead of copying its content.\n- Use `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` consistently.\n- State each instruction's scope explicitly to reduce ambiguity.\n\n## Examples: good and bad\n\n### Redundancy\n\n- **Good:** \"Update review criteria in `.pose/workflows/review.md` and reference that workflow from the root AGENTS file.\"\n- **Bad:** \"Repeat review criteria in AGENTS, the workflow, and every related spec.\"\n\n### Ambiguous reference\n\n- **Good:** \"Run the lint check described by the review workflow.\"\n- **Bad:** \"Run that standard validation before pushing.\"\n\n## Quick editorial checklist\n\n- Language is imperative and direct.\n- Bullets are short and non-overlapping.\n- Files do not duplicate sections.\n- `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` are used consistently.\n- References point to explicit files or paths.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/rules/knowledge-governance.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/rules/knowledge-governance.md'","aggregated_output":"# Rule: Knowledge Governance\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide when creating, updating, reviewing, or removing artifacts under `.pose/knowledge/`.\n\n## TTL and retention\n\n- Set `expires_at` on every artifact when it is created.\n- Use a default TTL of 30 days for notes, decision logs, and handoffs.\n- Use a TTL of up to 90 days only with a justification recorded in the artifact body.\n- Treat an artifact without `expires_at` as non-compliant and block its creation or merge.\n\n## When this is not the right home\n\n- Use `.pose/knowledge/` only for time-bound operational memory: content\n  tied to a decision moment, a handoff between executions, or something\n  meant to be reviewed and eventually expire.\n- A durable fact or convention that will not become false and carries no\n  review trigger — a third-party tool's parsing quirk, a naming\n  convention, a recurring gotcha — is not a `decision-log` just because no\n  better type exists. Route it to `.pose/rules/` instead: add it to an\n  existing rule file or create one, then wire it into the relevant task\n  type's base `rules` array in `.pose/indexes/task-map.json` so\n  `pose suggest <task>` surfaces it automatically. No path scoping is\n  required — a task type's base `rules` list is returned regardless of\n  `--path`; only language/component-specific rules need the\n  `rules_by_domain` layer.\n- Rule of thumb: if nothing was decided or traded off, and nothing about\n  the fact will change on a predictable timeline, it is not a\n  `decision-log` and not an ADR — it is a rule. See ADR\n  `2026-08-15-durable-non-architectural-knowledge-belongs-in-rules-not-a-new-type`.\n\n## Reusable cross-execution format\n\n- Structure reusable context as a handoff with fixed sections: Context, Current state, Next checks, Risks, and Next owner.\n- Keep `source_refs` linked to the relevant spec, workflow, and executed check commands.\n- Record `last_reviewed_at` in the body to make effective cross-execution updates traceable.\n\n## Archiving and purging\n\n- Run biweekly triage to list expired artifacts.\n- Move expired artifacts to `.pose/knowledge/archive/` when they retain audit value.\n- Purge archived artifacts 180 days after expiration unless documented legal or compliance requirements apply.\n- Record every archive and purge action in the housekeeping log.\n\n## Sensitive content\n\n- Prohibit secrets, tokens, credentials, private keys, and equivalent material.\n- Prohibit personal data and non-anonymized customer data.\n- Do not copy restricted incidents or reports in full; keep only a controlled reference.\n- Classify frontmatter `sensitivity` as `public-internal` or `restricted`.\n- Remove identified sensitive content immediately and open a security follow-up.\n\n## Ownership and review\n\n- Keep `@pose-maintainers` as the primary governance owner.\n- Require an owner in every artifact frontmatter.\n- Review expiration biweekly and quality monthly.\n- Escalate a backlog overdue by more than two cycles to the primary owner.\n- Block backlog growth when `list-expired` exceeds the operational limit: zero in strict mode, two in tolerant mode.\n\n## Minimum operational checks\n\n- Run `pose knowledge-check --strict` biweekly to validate the expired backlog.\n- Run `pose knowledge-housekeeping list-expired` for detailed triage.\n- Run `pose knowledge-housekeeping archive-expired --dry-run` before applying changes.\n- Execute destructive actions only with an explicit `--apply` flag.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
